A||||17||pontiacs4me||damn understeer almost got me killed!||||8||
Z||000000||pontiacs4me||06-08-2001||02:02 PM||||Yesterday I was driving home from helping my dad on one of his project houses.  I was getting ready to turn onto the highway entrance ramp. Just past the turn there is a median, and on the other side the exit road from the highway.  These turns are just on the other side of a small incline.  I was doing about 30 mph and figured I could make the turn no prob.  I turned the wheel and nothing happened, I started sliding toward the median.  So I straightened the wheel and slammed the brakes steering for the oncoming traffic.  No traffic was coming luckily.  My car skidded to a stop just to the grassy side of the exit ramp.  I cranked the wheel to the left, and gunned it, spun the rear end around and got back to the entrance side before anyone saw me.  At this time there was a car coming down the exit ramp.  If I was 30 seconds later I would of broadsided it, or it would of broadsided me.  Man I hate understeer.||||reg||8
Z||000001||Shiner||06-08-2001||02:46 PM||||I'm glad you and your car are still intact man.  Sounds to me like you had your foot too deep in the throttle though.  If you encounter understeer in a Fiero, typically lifting off the throttle somewhat/a lot will negate the understeer.  Just be carefull, and don't let the ass-end come too far around.<P>------------------<BR>-Shiner-<BR>'84 Sport Coupe<BR>'87 GT<BR>'86 SE 2M6 (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||06-08-2001||03:46 PM||||Even more alarming is the overstear(?) (loose rear) I get on one paticular turn around here. The turn just starts at the crest of a small hop in the road and bends to the right witch dipps down on the left hand side (off camber?). On rainy days I WILL not go faster than 15 MPH because I sucked up my seat cushion (9.5 pucker factor)one day when the cars ass end leapt out from under me leaving me sideways in the opposite lane. Fortunatly for me there was no oncoming traffic to contest. I am also at a disadvantage since my 2.5 lacks the throttle response and power to do any good on such short notice. I really hate those tires, GREAT dry traction but rally sucky in the wet<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||04:13 PM||||maybe you can explain the 'i turned the wheel and nothing happend' part a little more.<P>  the only time i would expect that is if you were already heavy on the brakes - if the front wheels are skidding then you cannot turn<P>  or maybe your front wheels were airborne?!<P>  or did you snap the steering wheel over so hard the traction broke loose and the tires skidded sideways?!<P>   Im having a hard time understanding exactly how you got into the situation from what you posted.<P>  the way you worded it, sounds as if your steering linkage had temporally disconnected.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pontiacs4me||06-08-2001||04:31 PM||||I turned the wheel, and the wheels turned, but skidded straight.  I think there was some sand or gravel there.  I wasnt on the brakes, or on the gas, I was just coasting into the turn.  Ive made turns that sharp at more than that speed before. I just think it was the coming off the incline, and some sand, caused me to skid the front tires.  It wasnt fun, but I laughed about it when I was all in the clear.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RiceCooker||06-08-2001||04:55 PM||||So, in fact it has nothing to do with understeer<P>Elementary, my dear RiceCooker||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||04:59 PM||||lets see, to prevent that from happening again you would need something like a 2000 psi tank of compressed air<P>and nozzels on the front of the car, with a switch on the dash labeled, 'sandhazard'<P>so you can blow the sand off the pavement before it gets under your wheels.<P>  thats one of the dangers of familiar roads - you know how fast you could take that turn last time, and you dont eyeball it like you would if it was the first time.<P>  I think I heard that most accidents happen within a mile of home or so, where we think we are on familiar territory.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||06-08-2001||08:46 PM||||I ran down in a ditch and into a fence doing the same thing.  I hit some sand the township puts down in the winter, and just pushed right through the corner.  I was like "WTF?"  I couldn't believe I skidded off the road at 20 mph.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||06-08-2001||09:00 PM||||I've always found it strange that when it's dry, my car has understeer but when it rains, it has oversteer. You would expect that when it rains, it would understeer more. Maybe it has something to do with the weight distribution?<P>Anyway, what I was wondering, I now have 205/60/15 all around, would 215/60/15 on the rear help get rid of the understeer (since the center of weight would be shifted slightly forward)? Or am I talking complete nonsense now (it <B>is</B> 3am here, and I <B>have</B> been TGIF-ing  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )?||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||06-08-2001||09:47 PM||||cant have both. When its dry with the larger tires on rear grip better (more surface) and push the front. When its wet, the back tires being wider hydroplane easier than front so rear gets loose or oversteers. Ideal for wet is front and rear matched (really only applies to 50/50 balanced or mid engine). For dry, you can fine tune the understeer or oversteer by changing the front and rear tire sizes. On my car, I find that 205/60 front and 225/60 rear makes the car pretty neutral dry. As for wet, I just slow down and make do.<P>I know what you mean Eric, lot of that grit around here too. Out in the boonies where I like to drive ive come around a curve 2 or 3 times over the posted safe speed, ive hit a spot where some doedoe has went off the road and threw dirt and sand on the road. Keeps life a little exciting.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||pontiacs4me||06-08-2001||11:29 PM||||Its partially understeer too, because Ive been in similiar situations in my firebird, and the ass end would of just kicked out I would of pointed the car into the entrance ramp and nailed it, and everything would of been fine.  BTW, im only 21 and consider myself an excellent driver, yeah yeah, ive only been driving for 6 years, but ive driven over 120,000 miles in that 6 years and Ive never been in a wreck or a situation I couldnt get myself out of.  And I drive like Luke Duke.  This episode was especially scary because its the first time Ive been out of control in the fiero.  Except for the night I spun out on ice, but that was intentional.  Anyway, thank god im alive and my car isnt hurt. <BR>One more thing what can I do to get this body roll thing under control?  Ive noticed when the car starts to understeer the body is rolling real bad, I think I need some polygraphite bushings.  Not to mention some 17's and a 1 1/2 inch drop.||||reg||1
Z||000011||87GT||06-09-2001||06:46 PM||||My good ole 87 doesnt like to turn sometimes when I turn the wheel either.  At low speeds, there is not a problem.  At higher speeds over 55, I can feel the car get lighter on turns and tend to act as if it wants to go straight rather than turn. Gotta love the 84-87 fieros.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tryxalon||06-09-2001||09:07 PM||||Well, the characteristics of a Firebird are a world away from the characteristics of a Fiero. <P>However, Pontiacs4me, you mentioned that there was something other than pavement as the surface you were driving on, and that there was excessive body roll during the manoever. I just tried to duplicate your experience ... and even at 50mph on dirt roads I could not induce a understeer that would match your experience. <P>I don't think it is excessive understeer.  And If it WAS, as soon as you got off the hammer and braked, the infamous "Fiero mid-engine Dropped Throttle Oversteer" would have moved you into the opposite condition.  <P>Sounds like you should check anti-sway bar control links, tie-rods, ball joints, steering arms, steering rack (possible worn pinion) and engine cradle bushings.<P> You apparently have <BR>        1) a car that is, as yet, beyond your skill (Improbable -- as Fiero's are quite easy to control.) <BR>     OR 2) (most likely) you have a mechanical suspension problem.<P>  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||84Bill||06-09-2001||11:00 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B><BR>Anyway, what I was wondering, I now have 205/60/15 all around, would 215/60/15 on the rear help get rid of the understeer (since the center of weight would be shifted slightly forward)? Or am I talking complete nonsense now (it [b]is</B> 3am here, and I <B>have</B> been TGIF-ing    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )?[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Now you got me thinking so I went out to check what type of tires I have. My 88 has Futura Euro 195 70 R14<BR>Traction A<BR>Treadwear 340<BR>Temp B<P>Theese tire stick well <P>My 84 has nearly the same tire<BR>Futura GLS 215 60 R14<BR>Traction A<BR>Tread 440 Harder?<BR>Temp B<BR>Theese tires scare me in this car.<BR>Both cars have about the same milage and tread depth on the tire<P>FYI I was coasting when I rounded the corner when the assend went out.<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroJoe||06-09-2001||11:53 PM||||The entrance to the highway I take to school everyday has a 25mph turn right in the beginning.  I usually take it at about 50 (unless there is someone in front of me who can't take it that fast), and I don't notice much body roll.  For being an 87, having 220,000 miles on the suspension, and having plain old carquest shocks, I have been very pleased with the way my car handles.  I know the car's limits now, and I have never encountered the trailing throttle oversteer.  Possibly just because I have a 2.5L engine.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000015||ray b||06-10-2001||12:22 AM||||litely on the brakes will help stick the front end by tranfering weight on to it.<BR>not too hard on the brakes just a little to move some weight up front.<BR>GPL grand prix legends is a very good sim<BR>and a free driving school down load the free demo of 67 gp cars pre-wings and learn with out killing yourself or your fiero on the computer.downloads at(URL=http://www.Legends Central)Legends Central(/URL)or (URL=http://www.Welcome to The Pits)Welcome to The Pits(/URL) or pappi,s site<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd<P>[This message has been edited by ray b (edited 06-11-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by ray b (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||pontiacs4me||06-10-2001||02:50 AM||||I think my whole front suspension is worn out.  Every time I back out of the drive way it makes some popping and banging noises unless I back down at the perfect angle.  <BR>More about my cars understeer.  I can be driving in a straight line, at 40 mph, on a flat, dry, perfect road, jerk the wheel real fast, say to change lanes, and the car will understeer a little.  Right when the car is at the peak of the roll, the front end feels like it is hopping, or skittering (for lack of a better word) across the pavement.  Oh well, guess it gives me something to work on, nothing else is going wrong.  Car is just too reliable.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Kwez||06-16-2001||06:53 PM||||hey ray b, <P>I'm a GPL fan, I can't put my finger on it exactly, but sometimes driving the Fiero reminds me of the feel of GPL (with Forcefeedback it's very nice).<BR>Usually coming out of a slow turn in the Fiero, under mild acceleration, the feel of 'weight in the back' I guess.<P>Wouldn't it be great if we could 'plug-in' the Fiero's numbers into the GPL sim ...||||reg||1
